77
00:02:58.380 --> 00:02:59.040
Fast Fact No.

78
00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.780
1, I said, this is written by Larry Wilmore.

79
00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:02.200
Mm-hmm.

80
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:08.180
You guys, I hope you remember, he played Mr. Brown in our Diversity Day episode.

81
00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:08.820
Yes.

82
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.320
Larry was a writer and producer and an amazing performer.

83
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:13.180
Yes.

84
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.340
And we are going to speak with Larry today.

85
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.700
Larry is so great.

86
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:18.680
I have such fond memories of working with him.

87
00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:20.980
You know, he created the Bernie Mac show.

88
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.560
He has been a correspondent on The Daily Show.

89
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.020
We've talked a little bit about this super talented guy.

90
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.500
I'm really excited to talk to him because he was just, he's so smart and funny.

91
00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.460
I think that was one of the things about our writer's room is like how freaking smart they were.

92
00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:37.780
They were really smart.

93
00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:42.020
I think people underestimate how smart you have to be to be a great writer.

94
00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:42.620
Yeah.

95
00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:44.160
Like they really go hand in hand.

96
00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.520
I think, I don't know.

97
00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.460
Sometimes I wonder, have we wasted all these minds?

98
00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:51.940
Should they be off making scientific discoveries?

99
00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.180
Because some of them were some just like the most brilliant people I've ever met.

100
00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.880
And they're just sitting writing great jokes.

101
00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.500
But then without them, we wouldn't have the fitness orb.

102
00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:00.340
Fast Fact No.

103
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.600
2, I was really sick in this episode.

104
00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.320
You were so sick.

105
00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:05.020
Do you remember that?

106
00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:10.920
Not only do I remember that you were sick, but I wrote down a quote that I read online.

107
00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.840
It says, where did you read this?

108
00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.260
On Wikipedia, on the Office Performance Review Wikipedia.

109
00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.980
It says, Jenna Fisher came down with a cold.

110
00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:25.700
Instead of postponing, however, Fisher memorized and performed all her lines, resulting in fellow

111
00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:29.060
cast member Angela Kinsey calling her a trooper.

112
00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.340
You were in this episode because you were really, really sick.

113
00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.020
But that's one thing I think you guys, I don't know if you guys know this, but when you're

114
00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.440
on a set and you get sick, you don't go home.

115
00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:39.980
No.

116
00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:41.220
They literally prop you up.

117
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.180
Because so many people, like what, 300 people?

118
00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.500
Their whole day revolves around that day's filming.

119
00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:46.920
Yeah.

120
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.300
And so it's sort of-

121
00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.520
They can't shut down the filming just because you don't feel good.

122
00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:50.960
Yeah.

123
00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.740
So a doctor will show up, give you a shot, and you stay at work.

124
00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:55.300
Oh, yeah.

125
00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.300
I mean, I've seen them tape people.

126
00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:01.540
They're like, oh, have you stopped vomiting for 30 minutes?

127
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.940
Let's get you on set and see what we can grab.

128
00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:04.500
Oh, yeah.

129
00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:05.300
It's like that happens.

130
00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:06.500
So I was really sick.

131
00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.700
This was my first time ever having to perform this ill.

132
00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:12.180
I took tons of Sudafed.

133
00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:14.120
I had a sinus infection.

134
00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:15.380
You were a little loopy.

135
00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:17.200
I was super loopy.

136
00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:19.560
Here's what's crazy.

137
00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:27.640
At the end of the week, Greg Daniels called my manager about my performance in this episode.

138
00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.660
And my manager called me.

139
00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:30.720
I get this message.

140
00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:31.780
Jenna, please call me.

141
00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:35.340
Greg Daniels has called about your performance in this week's episode, and I want to speak

142
00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:36.100
with you about it.

143
00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:37.480
Oh, shiitake mushrooMs.

144
00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:38.760
I thought I was being fired.

145
00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:39.120
Yeah.

146
00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:40.860
I thought, oh, my gosh.

147
00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:42.040
This is it.

148
00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:42.580
That's it.

149
00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:43.420
I don't know why.

150
00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:44.000
Did you-

151
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.440
I, as an actor, especially in the early days and on early shows, I always felt like

152
00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:49.660
I could be fired at any moment.

153
00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:53.820
Oh, me and Brian and Oscar were always like, accounting's totally replaceable.

154
00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.620
We could be fired at any time.

155
00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:56.420
Well, that's not true.

156
00:05:56.420 --> 00:06:01.000
But yes, you have this feeling like I'm failing constantly.

157
00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.020
So my manager says, Jenna, we got this phone call, and she got all my agents on the phone

158
00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:09.160
too, which you're like, oh, my gosh.

159
00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.300
They only do that if it's great news or horrible news.

160
00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.020
Or really bad news.

161
00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:18.480
So I'm like, and she's like, we received a phone call from Greg Daniels because he wanted

162
00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.640
all of us to know how outstanding you were in this episode, that it was some of your

163
00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:29.140
best work, and to please say, whatever it is you did to prepare for this episode, keep

164
00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:29.500
it up.

165
00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:31.720
Take Sudafed.

166
00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.640
I was like, I don't know what to do with this compliment.

167
00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:38.140
Clearly, I'm a-

168
00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:38.580
Thank you.

169
00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:42.100
Fantastic actress on Sudafed.

170
00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:42.560
Yeah.

171
00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:44.300
Well, okay.

172
00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:46.460
Okay, should I move on to fast fact number three?

